trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst did not provide the requested wound measurements or characteristics — likely because the data wasn’t available in the clinical record or wasn’t captured. Since physical exam findings are essential and *not optional*, and the prior [TEST] request yielded no response, I must re-request the most critical, high-yield element of the physical exam — but now with explicit emphasis on features that are pathognomonic for venous ulcer: location (especially medial malleolus), surrounding skin changes (hyperpigmentation, induration, atrophy), and pulse assessment. These distinguish venous from arterial or neuropathic ulcers and are required to complete Domain 7.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination of the right lower leg:  
- Wound location (e.g., medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, anterior tibia, calf);  
- Presence and extent of hyperpigmentation, lipodermatosclerosis, or atrophie blanche in surrounding skin;  
- Palpation of dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses (present/absent, diminished);  
- Assessment of edema (pitting/non-pitting, grade 1–3) and temperature comparison with left leg.

